(434636, 39008, 6999, '<td valign="top">Hi from the critique Club Jimmy.<br/> <br/>Er ... this is really a 10D photograph? what happened to the presence, the detail, the power of that camera? Why such a small edit? It seems grainy, over-sharpened, lacking in dynamic range ... rashed, perchance?<br/> <br/>There is, evidently, some detail here - the rings on the mini-dials'' faces are pretty clear, and there is some depth - viz the light on the edge of the sweep hand, but it''s a struggle to find those things. <br/> <br/>I don''t know whether your problems lie in post-processing, RAW file processing, or what. Don''t know what lens you used, and to be honest it would be beyond me to tell you what would be a good choice. One definite improvement would, I think, be to shoot at more of an angle - use the SLR''s facility with depth of field more.<br/> <br/>But still the submitted size and quality are a mystery given the camera ...<br/> <br/>ed</td>', 'Hi from the critique Club Jimmy. Er ... this is really a 10D photograph? what happened to the presence, the detail, the power of that camera? Why such a small edit? It seems grainy, over-sharpened, lacking in dynamic range ... rashed, perchance? There is, evidently, some detail here - the rings on the mini-dials'' faces are pretty clear, and there is some depth - viz the light on the edge of the sweep hand, but it''s a struggle to find those things.  I don''t know whether your problems lie in post-processing, RAW file processing, or what. Don''t know what lens you used, and to be honest it would be beyond me to tell you what would be a good choice. One definite improvement would, I think, be to shoot at more of an angle - use the SLR''s facility with depth of field more. But still the submitted size and quality are a mystery given the camera ... ed', '2003-09-29 19:47:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(434647, 38088, 7350, '<td valign="top">The detail and composition here are fantastic.  My favorite part, though, is the black background.  Did you bring this into a studio to shoot it?  Great shot!</td>', 'The detail and composition here are fantastic.  My favorite part, though, is the black background.  Did you bring this into a studio to shoot it?  Great shot!', '2003-09-29 19:51:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(434692, 38914, 7261, '<td valign="top">This is a great photograph, should have won</td>', 'This is a great photograph, should have won', '2003-09-29 20:33:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(434694, 38665, 11873, '<td valign="top">Hey, Congratulations!<br/> <br/>I guess we both like to look our subjects right in the eyes... all thirty-thousand of them!</td>', 'Hey, Congratulations! I guess we both like to look our subjects right in the eyes... all thirty-thousand of them!', '2003-09-29 20:36:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(434867, 38895, 10404, '<td valign="top">In reply to the comments of my use of catch lights in the doll''s eyes: I had nothing to do with it - They were actually painted on "at the factory".  Thanks for everyone''s comments on my first submission!</td>', 'In reply to the comments of my use of catch lights in the doll''s eyes: I had nothing to do with it - They were actually painted on "at the factory".  Thanks for everyone''s comments on my first submission!', '2003-09-30 00:16:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(434923, 38109, 12204, '<td valign="top">I''m actually very happy with all of your responses. Even the negative responses acknowledged the effect I was looking for.<br/> <br/>For those of you who had asked about it.<br/> <br/>It''s not as close as you may think. It was a 5 megapixel picture of a steel lawn sculpture that is a mouse. It was cropped at 640x480 almost exactly without having to resize it. After I had cropped the picture of the mouse to get the most interesting picture, I discovered the cold blue tones in it. This led me to play with the contrast, brightness, and color till I got just the right tonal image I wanted. <br/> <br/>I called it the mouse trap because it''s a closeup picture of the steel mouses and with all of the springs and such, it reminded me of a broken mouse trap. If I hadn''t called it mouse trap, it would of been called cold blue steel, but I figured mouse trap was a better name after some debate as cold blue steel would have implied a crisper and sharper image.</td>', 'I''m actually very happy with all of your responses. Even the negative responses acknowledged the effect I was looking for. For those of you who had asked about it. It''s not as close as you may think. It was a 5 megapixel picture of a steel lawn sculpture that is a mouse. It was cropped at 640x480 almost exactly without having to resize it. After I had cropped the picture of the mouse to get the most interesting picture, I discovered the cold blue tones in it. This led me to play with the contrast, brightness, and color till I got just the right tonal image I wanted.  I called it the mouse trap because it''s a closeup picture of the steel mouses and with all of the springs and such, it reminded me of a broken mouse trap. If I hadn''t called it mouse trap, it would of been called cold blue steel, but I figured mouse trap was a better name after some debate as cold blue steel would have implied a crisper and sharper image.', '2003-09-30 00:57:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(434925, 38088, 6870, '<td valign="top">Actually, it was on my front porch in front of a black foam core board. Sunlight is my favorite light to work with these days!</td>', 'Actually, it was on my front porch in front of a black foam core board. Sunlight is my favorite light to work with these days!', '2003-09-30 00:57:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(435227, 38628, 917, '<td valign="top">Its a hoverfly of the Syphus genus (E.g. Syrphus ribesii) its impossible to tell the exact species from the photo, you''d need a better shot of the wing to be sure. <br/>Its in Alaska, so perhaps there are only a couple of species recorded in that area, and you can get a good idea of species by eliminating all lookalikes from elsewhere.</td>', 'Its a hoverfly of the Syphus genus (E.g. Syrphus ribesii) its impossible to tell the exact species from the photo, you''d need a better shot of the wing to be sure. Its in Alaska, so perhaps there are only a couple of species recorded in that area, and you can get a good idea of species by eliminating all lookalikes from elsewhere.', '2003-09-30 09:19:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(435282, 38981, 10985, '<td valign="top">A drop of water added close to the wick? This is very cool.</td>', 'A drop of water added close to the wick? This is very cool.', '2003-09-30 11:13:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(435372, 38807, 714, '<td valign="top">Still one of my favorites!  Love it!</td>', 'Still one of my favorites!  Love it!', '2003-09-30 13:49:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(435387, 38856, 4482, '<td valign="top">One of my tops! Those cliché super macro lens guys beat ya! snif snif!<br/> <br/>Great job!</td>', 'One of my tops! Those cliché super macro lens guys beat ya! snif snif! Great job!', '2003-09-30 14:07:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(435521, 38628, 6349, '<td valign="top">Thanks UberFish!</td>', 'Thanks UberFish!', '2003-09-30 17:07:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(435671, 38227, 3290, '<td valign="top">Hi Panos, I thought this shot deserved to do better - it was one of my highest-rated for the challenge. I really like the colours of the thread and the reflections in the spools, as well as the geometric patterns. Keep up the good work.</td>', 'Hi Panos, I thought this shot deserved to do better - it was one of my highest-rated for the challenge. I really like the colours of the thread and the reflections in the spools, as well as the geometric patterns. Keep up the good work.', '2003-09-30 19:43:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(435715, 38963, 12042, '<td valign="top">wow that''s amazing. congratulations</td>', 'wow that''s amazing. congratulations', '2003-09-30 20:14:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(435718, 38665, 12042, '<td valign="top">yuck!!! but still a amazing picture.. congratulations</td>', 'yuck!!! but still a amazing picture.. congratulations', '2003-09-30 20:17:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(435744, 38721, 11034, '<td valign="top">Muy buena foto Genaro. Mucho detalle y llena de color. Saludos</td>', 'Muy buena foto Genaro. Mucho detalle y llena de color. Saludos', '2003-09-30 20:37:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(435965, 38981, 2667, '<td valign="top">Ok, for those that wish to know how this was done, here goes... It''s really nothing more than a pimped out version of an old parlour trick. The one where you take a lighter and roll the wheel slowly so as not to ignite the flint, then tap the flint onto a flame or other heat source and it will "explode" in midair like you see here. It took me over 50 frames to get everything right. 1/2 second exposures made the timing rather tricky. The full frame version (much more interesting, if you ask me) can be seen here: //www.pbase.com/image/21357553/original</td>', 'Ok, for those that wish to know how this was done, here goes... It''s really nothing more than a pimped out version of an old parlour trick. The one where you take a lighter and roll the wheel slowly so as not to ignite the flint, then tap the flint onto a flame or other heat source and it will "explode" in midair like you see here. It took me over 50 frames to get everything right. 1/2 second exposures made the timing rather tricky. The full frame version (much more interesting, if you ask me) can be seen here: //www.pbase.com/image/21357553/original', '2003-10-01 01:00:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(436210, 38814, 10084, '<td valign="top">Reflection on the syringe is a tiny bit distracting. I would have like to see the point in the needle. I think that it would help to give more impact to the shot.</td>', 'Reflection on the syringe is a tiny bit distracting. I would have like to see the point in the needle. I think that it would help to give more impact to the shot.', '2003-10-01 06:26:26.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(436623, 38283, 10985, '<td valign="top">Now this is one superb macro shot. Very unexpected and so very close and sharp! I had given you a 10, probably the only one I''ve given here yet. I don''t regret a thing.</td>', 'Now this is one superb macro shot. Very unexpected and so very close and sharp! I had given you a 10, probably the only one I''ve given here yet. I don''t regret a thing.', '2003-10-01 11:02:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(437040, 37996, 10848, '<td valign="top">OMG! This is a gorgeous shot! IMHO this is one of the best floral macro shots I have ever seen..I certainly do appreciate the artistic essence displayed in this shot..I hope you continue to do well..if this shot is any indication you are certainly doing great...congrats on this shot! Great job!</td>', 'OMG! This is a gorgeous shot! IMHO this is one of the best floral macro shots I have ever seen..I certainly do appreciate the artistic essence displayed in this shot..I hope you continue to do well..if this shot is any indication you are certainly doing great...congrats on this shot! Great job!', '2003-10-01 14:47:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(437604, 38782, 6870, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I concur with all of the positive statements below!<br/> <br/>In this image, these peppers really do resemble fingers. It seems to me to be, ultimately, an abstract shot, and this is further emphasized by your narrow depth of field and lack of any one or group of peppers which has sharp focus. <br/> <br/>The use of many diagonal lines give the photo some interest and drama, and the fact that they are randomly diagonal and go off in many directions provides a more natural feel.<br/> <br/>You have made some interesting lighting choices here, too. Some peppers are quite harshly lit and overexposed, and this adds to the feeling of randomness and abstractness I feel when I study it. Most of the lighting, however, is warm and friendly and interesting and help to add shape and form to the peppers.<br/> <br/>I find few distractions in this photograph. I like the way my eye wanders around and investigates the forms, colors and shapes found in it. Had you had the entire bulk of peppers in focus, it would have been distracting, so I agree with your choice of soft focus on everything, for I feel it contributes to the abstract look. Since we can still tell quite well that these are peppers, however, the abstract nature is somewhat undermined, thus leading to the discrepancy in some voters'' minds that at least SOMETHING should have been in focus. One suggestion, that may or may not be the right one here, is to make sure that if you break a rule, break it enthusiastically so everyone knows that it was a deliberate choice and not a sloppy oversight.<br/> <br/>Again, nice work! I really enjoy this! If you have any questions, just ask!<br/> <br/>David</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I concur with all of the positive statements below! In this image, these peppers really do resemble fingers. It seems to me to be, ultimately, an abstract shot, and this is further emphasized by your narrow depth of field and lack of any one or group of peppers which has sharp focus.  The use of many diagonal lines give the photo some interest and drama, and the fact that they are randomly diagonal and go off in many directions provides a more natural feel. You have made some interesting lighting choices here, too. Some peppers are quite harshly lit and overexposed, and this adds to the feeling of randomness and abstractness I feel when I study it. Most of the lighting, however, is warm and friendly and interesting and help to add shape and form to the peppers. I find few distractions in this photograph. I like the way my eye wanders around and investigates the forms, colors and shapes found in it. Had you had the entire bulk of peppers in focus, it would have been distracting, so I agree with your choice of soft focus on everything, for I feel it contributes to the abstract look. Since we can still tell quite well that these are peppers, however, the abstract nature is somewhat undermined, thus leading to the discrepancy in some voters'' minds that at least SOMETHING should have been in focus. One suggestion, that may or may not be the right one here, is to make sure that if you break a rule, break it enthusiastically so everyone knows that it was a deliberate choice and not a sloppy oversight. Again, nice work! I really enjoy this! If you have any questions, just ask! David', '2003-10-01 21:02:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(437610, 38951, 6870, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I concur with all of the positive comments below!<br/> <br/>In this image, I see a unique presentation of a flower, created with rainbow erasers. The rainbow erasers are enough of a clue to me that this is a photo with a bit of whimsy. The colors you''ve chosen--especially the red background--work well to emphasize this further.<br/> <br/>It looks like some folks did not think this was a macro shot and scored you lower, but we won''t go there, since I''m not so sure when a normal shot ends and when a ''macro'' shot begins.<br/> <br/>The funky shape of the stem reinforces the whimsy feel to the photo. I like the negative space on the right; to me it implies that there are other funky things in the world of this groovy flower.<br/> <br/>I find your colors fun, but slightly desaturated and hazy; you may have lost some color to the web here, or you may want to boost them, and the darker tones in your image, to give a feeling of more clarity.<br/> <br/>This is a fun shot that is true to itself!<br/> <br/>Best of luck for the future!<br/> <br/>David<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I concur with all of the positive comments below! In this image, I see a unique presentation of a flower, created with rainbow erasers. The rainbow erasers are enough of a clue to me that this is a photo with a bit of whimsy. The colors you''ve chosen--especially the red background--work well to emphasize this further. It looks like some folks did not think this was a macro shot and scored you lower, but we won''t go there, since I''m not so sure when a normal shot ends and when a ''macro'' shot begins. The funky shape of the stem reinforces the whimsy feel to the photo. I like the negative space on the right; to me it implies that there are other funky things in the world of this groovy flower. I find your colors fun, but slightly desaturated and hazy; you may have lost some color to the web here, or you may want to boost them, and the darker tones in your image, to give a feeling of more clarity. This is a fun shot that is true to itself! Best of luck for the future! David ', '2003-10-01 21:11:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(437920, 39000, 12736, '<td valign="top">this is highly creative and imaginative I really enjoyed the entire idea of it as well as the really wonderful colors that you  always seem to see in CD''s and dvd''d, wonderfully done,<br/>kindest regards,<br/>joe</td>', 'this is highly creative and imaginative I really enjoyed the entire idea of it as well as the really wonderful colors that you  always seem to see in CD''s and dvd''d, wonderfully done,kindest regards,joe', '2003-10-02 05:19:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(438010, 38283, 2780, '<td valign="top">Congrats jacko!</td>', 'Congrats jacko!', '2003-10-02 08:21:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(438012, 38665, 2780, '<td valign="top">wow crab, great macro, congrats!</td>', 'wow crab, great macro, congrats!', '2003-10-02 08:22:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(438013, 38963, 2780, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the blue ribbon, great macro</td>', 'Congrats on the blue ribbon, great macro', '2003-10-02 08:23:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(438219, 38010, 9452, '<td valign="top">I was really expecting a ribbon for this one, it was one of the very few 10. But it doesn''t matter, i bet you inspired a lot of people like me to experiment on shots like this. Keep shooting that nice! ;)</td>', 'I was really expecting a ribbon for this one, it was one of the very few 10. But it doesn''t matter, i bet you inspired a lot of people like me to experiment on shots like this. Keep shooting that nice! ;)', '2003-10-02 13:37:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(438263, 38734, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>This is a really nice shot.  I really like the detail that you show us in the acorns.  Very nice focus helps the detail to stand out and grab out attention.<br/>I do really like the angle and the framing/cropping are nice as well.  You have placed the horizontal line (branch) very nicely within the photo.  I like how it doesn''t draw our eyes out of the photo, but rather draws our eyes right to the acorns.<br/>Lighting for the most part is really good.  If I had to make a suggestion about this photo, I would suggest to try to cut out the white areas around the left edges of the acorns.  They seem a bit harsh, but really don''t affect the overall impact of the photo.  I think this is really nicely done.<br/>Great DOF.  The background is nicely blurred as to not be a distraction to the subject, but there is a bright spot just to the right of the subject, that could distract slightly.<br/>Overall very nice. <br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*This is a really nice shot.  I really like the detail that you show us in the acorns.  Very nice focus helps the detail to stand out and grab out attention.I do really like the angle and the framing/cropping are nice as well.  You have placed the horizontal line (branch) very nicely within the photo.  I like how it doesn''t draw our eyes out of the photo, but rather draws our eyes right to the acorns.Lighting for the most part is really good.  If I had to make a suggestion about this photo, I would suggest to try to cut out the white areas around the left edges of the acorns.  They seem a bit harsh, but really don''t affect the overall impact of the photo.  I think this is really nicely done.Great DOF.  The background is nicely blurred as to not be a distraction to the subject, but there is a bright spot just to the right of the subject, that could distract slightly.Overall very nice. ~Heather~', '2003-10-02 14:39:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(438279, 38856, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>You got so many great comments on this shot already, that I''m not really sure if anything I would have to say would really help.<br/>I do really like this shot for a few reasons.  First, your focus and clarity are really great, which increases the wonderful detail we see in both the leaf and the water.<br/>The color is great.  Nice bright leaf against a fairly colorless background really helps for the leaf to stand out and grab us.<br/>The angle and framing/cropping are ok.  I like that this is a vertical crop and not horizontal.  I think that really makes the lines ''flow'' better. No pun intended. lol.<br/>I''m not usually a fan of slow shutter speed water, but this is so very mild that it really does look good.  It''s when it looks like milk that it kind of disturbs me.<br/>Anyway, you have done a wonderful job with this shot, and I think it deserves the high score.<br/>Great shooting. <br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*You got so many great comments on this shot already, that I''m not really sure if anything I would have to say would really help.I do really like this shot for a few reasons.  First, your focus and clarity are really great, which increases the wonderful detail we see in both the leaf and the water.The color is great.  Nice bright leaf against a fairly colorless background really helps for the leaf to stand out and grab us.The angle and framing/cropping are ok.  I like that this is a vertical crop and not horizontal.  I think that really makes the lines ''flow'' better. No pun intended. lol.I''m not usually a fan of slow shutter speed water, but this is so very mild that it really does look good.  It''s when it looks like milk that it kind of disturbs me.Anyway, you have done a wonderful job with this shot, and I think it deserves the high score.Great shooting. ~Heather~', '2003-10-02 15:12:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(438283, 38458, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>I think that the thought is here.  The spider is definately interesting to me.  I like that he''s on a simple background.<br/>The colors seem dull and muted to me though.  By the harsh highlight on his back, i''m assuming that this is because of the lighting.  Saturation and contrast adjustments could definately help to bring this out.<br/>Ideally, a different time of the day might be a better decision, however, with live animals, you can''t exactly ask them to come back at a different time, so sometimes you take what you can get.<br/>The focus is a bit soft, which could be from the lighting, or it could also be that maybe it was moving slightly?<br/>I think that the best way to get a great shot of something like this is to take LOTS of shots.  It''s digital, so we don''t have to worry about wasting film.  Just go crazy, take like 70 shots of the exact same thing.  At least one is bound to be in focus, or have just the right angle.  Then you can mess with color and contrast later in an editing program.<br/>The angle is ok.  Maybe a bit too head on, but still not bad.  I do like how you have blurred the background, nothing distracting there.  Overall, crispness, and color would have made this shot really good.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*I think that the thought is here.  The spider is definately interesting to me.  I like that he''s on a simple background.The colors seem dull and muted to me though.  By the harsh highlight on his back, i''m assuming that this is because of the lighting.  Saturation and contrast adjustments could definately help to bring this out.Ideally, a different time of the day might be a better decision, however, with live animals, you can''t exactly ask them to come back at a different time, so sometimes you take what you can get.The focus is a bit soft, which could be from the lighting, or it could also be that maybe it was moving slightly?I think that the best way to get a great shot of something like this is to take LOTS of shots.  It''s digital, so we don''t have to worry about wasting film.  Just go crazy, take like 70 shots of the exact same thing.  At least one is bound to be in focus, or have just the right angle.  Then you can mess with color and contrast later in an editing program.The angle is ok.  Maybe a bit too head on, but still not bad.  I do like how you have blurred the background, nothing distracting there.  Overall, crispness, and color would have made this shot really good.~Heather~', '2003-10-02 15:22:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(438290, 38667, 3080, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/>While I do appreciate different aproaches to things, I don''t think that it works well for this shot in my opinion.<br/>I do have to agree with the others that I think it would have been more effective with the focus more towards the front of the shot, or either have all of the beans in focus.<br/>If you did the focus this way because you thought that the beans in the back were more interesting, than you could have shot from an opposite angle.<br/>Your focus is really good in the back though, we get to see lots of nice detail and sharp lines, and I like that.  I''m a huge fan of detail, and we get to see a lot of that here.<br/>I like the arrangement of the beans.  I think that you seperated them well except for one little orange bean that sneaked into the white beans near the front.  He''s bothering me a little cause he''s so very much out of place there.  shame on him.<br/>Overall, I think it''s a great subject for macro.<br/>~Heather~</td>', '*Critique Club*While I do appreciate different aproaches to things, I don''t think that it works well for this shot in my opinion.I do have to agree with the others that I think it would have been more effective with the focus more towards the front of the shot, or either have all of the beans in focus.If you did the focus this way because you thought that the beans in the back were more interesting, than you could have shot from an opposite angle.Your focus is really good in the back though, we get to see lots of nice detail and sharp lines, and I like that.  I''m a huge fan of detail, and we get to see a lot of that here.I like the arrangement of the beans.  I think that you seperated them well except for one little orange bean that sneaked into the white beans near the front.  He''s bothering me a little cause he''s so very much out of place there.  shame on him.Overall, I think it''s a great subject for macro.~Heather~', '2003-10-02 15:31:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(438391, 38655, 6870, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I concur with all of the positive statements below!<br/> <br/>I''m excited because this was one of my favorite shots of this challenge! This is an image full of drama and has a real magic to it, I feel. The point of view with the fingers coming at the viewer is very engaging and adds a lot of interest. It is also a unique photo, and has a lot of originality.<br/> <br/>Your careful attention to lighting really paid off here. The ball is well lit, and the colors are intense. A few mentioned the glare on the ball, and I don''t mind glare if it serves a function; this glare gives the glass ball some intense highlights that help define it and also adds more drama to the shot. If it were not there, I don''t think the shot would work as well. I suppose you could experiment and see how it looks by burning it a little.<br/> <br/>The composition really works. An object this exciting certainly deserves to be front and center!<br/> <br/>Wonderful stuff--an excellent shot in a very competitive challenge! Keep up the good work!<br/> <br/>David</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I concur with all of the positive statements below! I''m excited because this was one of my favorite shots of this challenge! This is an image full of drama and has a real magic to it, I feel. The point of view with the fingers coming at the viewer is very engaging and adds a lot of interest. It is also a unique photo, and has a lot of originality. Your careful attention to lighting really paid off here. The ball is well lit, and the colors are intense. A few mentioned the glare on the ball, and I don''t mind glare if it serves a function; this glare gives the glass ball some intense highlights that help define it and also adds more drama to the shot. If it were not there, I don''t think the shot would work as well. I suppose you could experiment and see how it looks by burning it a little. The composition really works. An object this exciting certainly deserves to be front and center! Wonderful stuff--an excellent shot in a very competitive challenge! Keep up the good work! David', '2003-10-02 18:11:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(438437, 38260, 6999, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club ...<br/> <br/>Well, evidently people think of Macros as hyper-close-ups, and there is an argument that says it ''really'' means images larger than actual life, but whatever ... you certainly fulfill the specifics of the challenge - it''s just others'' opinions of what counts that''s cost you in the voting.<br/> <br/>There''s a ''rule'' in portraits, even of animals, that the secret of taking good shots is to have the eyes in focus, and you haven''t quite got that here: whether it''s a slight movement from the animal, or a slight camera shake, or just slightly off focus I can''t tell, though i''d guess one of the former two. <br/> <br/>enough DOF to put the background nicely out of focus, though i think if you could have caught it against a darker surrrounding that would also have helped. The light is also a little flat - not bad, really, but I think a touch more definition and shape from more directional light would also have helped. <br/> <br/>The final result is underservnigly low though: these faults aren''t so great as the score would suggest to me; though likewise, it isn''t an unimprovable photograph.<br/> <br/>HTH<br/> <br/>Ed</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club ... Well, evidently people think of Macros as hyper-close-ups, and there is an argument that says it ''really'' means images larger than actual life, but whatever ... you certainly fulfill the specifics of the challenge - it''s just others'' opinions of what counts that''s cost you in the voting. There''s a ''rule'' in portraits, even of animals, that the secret of taking good shots is to have the eyes in focus, and you haven''t quite got that here: whether it''s a slight movement from the animal, or a slight camera shake, or just slightly off focus I can''t tell, though i''d guess one of the former two.  enough DOF to put the background nicely out of focus, though i think if you could have caught it against a darker surrrounding that would also have helped. The light is also a little flat - not bad, really, but I think a touch more definition and shape from more directional light would also have helped.  The final result is underservnigly low though: these faults aren''t so great as the score would suggest to me; though likewise, it isn''t an unimprovable photograph. HTH Ed', '2003-10-02 19:08:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(438618, 38283, 714, '<td valign="top">wow!  Great shot.  Congrats, Jacko!  You are the king of Macros!</td>', 'wow!  Great shot.  Congrats, Jacko!  You are the king of Macros!', '2003-10-03 00:59:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(438985, 38857, 3889, '<td valign="top">Wow, a pretty amazing photo.  I do like the background color and the different perspective for the side (I hope) instead of head on.  For these critters, you did a great job (but I still hate bugs).<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Sandy</td>', 'Wow, a pretty amazing photo.  I do like the background color and the different perspective for the side (I hope) instead of head on.  For these critters, you did a great job (but I still hate bugs). Regards, Sandy', '2003-10-03 16:11:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(439214, 38134, 811, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>Gordon, This shot is very well done, as usual, and I love the way the blue and yellow contrast eachother.  The very sharp focus really grabs the eyes.  The only thing "wrong" is that the white part at the bottom is realy bright, and seems a bit overexposed on this monitor.  At work, where the monitor is slightly darker, it was just right.<br/> <br/>Great work.<br/>karmat</td>', 'Critique Club Gordon, This shot is very well done, as usual, and I love the way the blue and yellow contrast eachother.  The very sharp focus really grabs the eyes.  The only thing "wrong" is that the white part at the bottom is realy bright, and seems a bit overexposed on this monitor.  At work, where the monitor is slightly darker, it was just right. Great work.karmat', '2003-10-04 00:22:37.000000', NULL, 0),
