(439901, 38996, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Very interesting macro.  It has a lot of potential. The compositing is good the problem is the lighting was just to poor and ruined a beautiful photo.  I''d love to see this done over with better lighting.<br/> <br/>It meets the challenge, just watch out for shadows.  Good attempt.</td>', '*Critique Club* Very interesting macro.  It has a lot of potential. The compositing is good the problem is the lighting was just to poor and ruined a beautiful photo.  I''d love to see this done over with better lighting. It meets the challenge, just watch out for shadows.  Good attempt.', '2003-10-05 21:57:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(439975, 38109, 811, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>To be perfectly honest, this was a shot that just didn''t hole my attention  long enough to try and figure out what you were getting at.  After reading your comments, I trust you were pleased with the outcome, but I still could not see exactly where you were going with it.  I guess in some ways that is the purpose of an abstract piece, and in that respect, you did well.<br/> <br/>I think compositionally, if there had been more of the subject in the shot, perhaps lower and to the left, it would have seemed more balanced to me.  The spring, or whatever the coil is on the right, is interesting, and provides a natural resting spot for the eyes, then leads them up and down, so perhaps that could have been given a more prominent role in the picture.<br/> <br/>I think if you could have gotten the colors, etc. that you have without the extra noise, it would have been better.  As it is, it kinda looks like you post-processed excessively to cover up some inherent flaw in the picture.<br/> <br/>I know it sounds like I am totally downing your shot, but I promise that is not the case.  This is the kind of shot I would show my English students and have them do a creative writing assignment on.  As a photograph, it just doesn''t appeal to me personally, though I do think it is an excellent example of abstract that causes people to wonder, adn leaves things open to interpretation.<br/> <br/>Best wishes in future challenges.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'Critique Clubby karmat To be perfectly honest, this was a shot that just didn''t hole my attention  long enough to try and figure out what you were getting at.  After reading your comments, I trust you were pleased with the outcome, but I still could not see exactly where you were going with it.  I guess in some ways that is the purpose of an abstract piece, and in that respect, you did well. I think compositionally, if there had been more of the subject in the shot, perhaps lower and to the left, it would have seemed more balanced to me.  The spring, or whatever the coil is on the right, is interesting, and provides a natural resting spot for the eyes, then leads them up and down, so perhaps that could have been given a more prominent role in the picture. I think if you could have gotten the colors, etc. that you have without the extra noise, it would have been better.  As it is, it kinda looks like you post-processed excessively to cover up some inherent flaw in the picture. I know it sounds like I am totally downing your shot, but I promise that is not the case.  This is the kind of shot I would show my English students and have them do a creative writing assignment on.  As a photograph, it just doesn''t appeal to me personally, though I do think it is an excellent example of abstract that causes people to wonder, adn leaves things open to interpretation. Best wishes in future challenges. karmat', '2003-10-05 23:47:13.000000', NULL, 0),
